      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix",
      "problem": "If $Q=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+5|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 3)+2(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))+5(4\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =3(2)+2(8)+5(12+1) \\\\  & =6+16+5(13) \\\\  & =6+16+65 \\\\  & =87\\end{aligned}\n$$",
