      "math_academy_id": "q-280394",
      "knowledge_point_id": "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix",
      "problem": "If $R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-1))+2(4\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 6)+0 \\\\  & =1(6+5)+2(8-30) \\\\  & =11+2(-22) \\\\  & =11-44 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$",
