      "knowledge_point_id": "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant",
      "problem": "Given that the determinant of  $[\\begin{aligned}1 & n & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is $-6$, find the value of $n.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & n & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4-n\\cdot 2+0 \\\\  & =4-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-6,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2n & =-6 \\\\ -2n & =-10 \\\\ n & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
